				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>STIGRef</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Web Server Security Requirements Guide :: Version 4, Release: 5 Benchmark Date: 01 Jul 2026</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>TargetKey</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA />
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>STIG_UUID</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>24c0e41e-1385-59de-8d1a-906595a01495</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>CCI_REF</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>CCI-000054</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STATUS>NotAFinding</STATUS>
				<FINDING_DETAILS>MITIGATED. Three ceilings, all bounded rather than generous: the ingress bounds simultaneous in-flight requests and HTTP/2 streams, nginx bounds connections per source address, and request HEADER capacity is bounded by capping per-request CSRF cookies at the SSO gate instead of enlarging the server's header buffers. Header buffers are per-connection memory and every analyst shares one source address through the broker, so sizing them to absorb unbounded cookie accumulation would have weakened the per-source bound to hide a fixable defect.

Evidence: traefik/dynamic-sso/dynamic.yml (inflight), traefik/traefik.yml (http2.maxConcurrentStreams), frontend/nginx.conf.template (limit_conn, large_client_header_buffers 4 16k), deploy/enclave/docker-compose.yml (--cookie-csrf-per-request-limit)

Verified by: test/uat_srg_webtier.sh — connection and stream limits asserted as deployed; the CSRF cookie cap is asserted at the gate and the header buffer asserted to remain at its bounded value, so a regression that widens it to absorb accumulation fails the test</FINDING_DETAILS>
				<COMMENTS>Determination: Mitigated. Tracked in artifacts/srg_status.yml; regenerate with gen_srg_tracker.py.</COMMENTS>
				<SEVERITY_OVERRIDE />
				<SEVERITY_JUSTIFICATION />
			</VULN>
