					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Class</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Unclass</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>STIGRef</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Web Server Security Requirements Guide :: Version 4, Release: 5 Benchmark Date: 01 Jul 2026</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>TargetKey</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA />
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>STIG_UUID</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>24c0e41e-1385-59de-8d1a-906595a01495</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>CCI_REF</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>CCI-000054</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STATUS>NotAFinding</STATUS>
				<FINDING_DETAILS>MITIGATED. Sessions are held server-side in Redis, not in the client's cookie, so signing out or canceling a session ends it for real. The gate is a mesh service in its own right: Redis binds loopback only, so the gate reaches it through its own Connect sidecar and its access is an intention rather than network reachability. Database 1 keeps analyst sessions out of the task broker's keyspace.

Evidence: deploy/enclave/docker-compose.yml (oauth2-proxy --session-store-type=redis, oauth2-proxy- sidecar), hashicorp/consul/config-entries/ir-redis.hcl, hashicorp/consul/register-mesh.sh

Verified by: test/uat_srg_webtier.sh — a real authorization-code login through the ingress grows the session database from 0 to 1 key; with a cookie store that count never moves</FINDING_DETAILS>
				<COMMENTS>Determination: Mitigated. Tracked in artifacts/srg_status.yml; regenerate with gen_srg_tracker.py.</COMMENTS>
				<SEVERITY_OVERRIDE />
				<SEVERITY_JUSTIFICATION />
