				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>STIGRef</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Web Server Security Requirements Guide :: Version 4, Release: 5 Benchmark Date: 01 Jul 2026</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>TargetKey</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA />
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>STIG_UUID</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>24c0e41e-1385-59de-8d1a-906595a01495</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>CCI_REF</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>CCI-002470</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STATUS>Open</STATUS>
				<FINDING_DETAILS>PLANNED — feasible on the current architecture.

The PLUMBING is built and inert: hashicorp/keycloak/pki-logon/ holds the x509 browser flow, a trust-anchor allow-list and a CRL directory, and deploy.sh renders both the ingress client-auth block and the realm flow from IR_PKI_LOGON — rendering the DISABLED form explicitly, so a deployment that once had it on stops asking for certificates. Enabling it with no trust anchor is refused, because that is a lockout rather than a hardening step. What remains is not code: an issuing CA, cards carrying its certificates, and revocation data staged into the enclave. It stays opt-in until an environment has them.

Where: hashicorp/keycloak/pki-logon/, deploy/deploy.sh (pki_logon_render), traefik/dynamic- sso/dynamic.yml, deploy/.env (IR_PKI_LOGON)</FINDING_DETAILS>
				<COMMENTS>Determination: Implement. Tracked in artifacts/srg_status.yml; regenerate with gen_srg_tracker.py.</COMMENTS>
