				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>CCI_REF</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>CCI-004922</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STATUS>NotAFinding</STATUS>
				<FINDING_DETAILS>MITIGATED. The enclave runs its own chrony, staged beside the resolver and for the same reason: `internal: true` installs no route off the host, so both authorities must live inside the segment. It serves the enclave HOSTS, whose chrony clients discipline their own clocks; containers inherit from the host and cannot set time, so the service runs with -x and never touches a clock it does not own. This matters more here than on an ordinary web tier: temporal_coherence compares instants ACROSS hosts, so a drifting enclave does not produce a wrong-looking record, it places a plausible one at the wrong point on the timeline.

Evidence: ntp/Dockerfile, ntp/chrony.conf, ntp/entrypoint.sh, deploy/enclave/docker-compose.yml (ntp on internal + dmzlink), deploy/deploy.sh stage 0a, ci/clock-sync-check.sh

Verified by: uat_srg_webtier.sh — the service answers chronyc and is serving; the host clock is synchronized; and a container's clock is proven to agree with the host's, which is the inheritance the design assumed and had never tested</FINDING_DETAILS>
				<COMMENTS>Determination: Mitigated. Tracked in artifacts/srg_status.yml; regenerate with gen_srg_tracker.py.</COMMENTS>
				<SEVERITY_OVERRIDE />
				<SEVERITY_JUSTIFICATION />
